              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing survey data**\n\nI’m thinking about how to approach this analysis. Each analyst looks at three distinct surveys, and it seems each observation should only count once. So, with six analysts, that totals 18 observations. Peer reports might be helping infer their observations, but the confidence levels don’t quite match up. \n\nMaybe the model isn’t a standard posterior? I'm curious about how confidence should relate to counts, considering shared demand might complicate interpretation. I’m trying to figure out how to use these confidence values effectively!**Calculating report reliability**\n\nI'm considering how to calculate predictive values based on peer reports and their reliability. If P(report answer equals demand) equals reliability 'c,' with a prior of .5, the likelihood ratio becomes c/(1-c). This method assumes 'c' is the reliability, but I need to remember the warnings about probabilistic use.\n\nWith my own three high reports giving me a p of .7714, it seems peer reports may help through Bayesian updating. I wonder if their confidence might also be conditioned on data, affecting results. Now, I need to compute accuracy using the reported correctness values!**Calculating odds and ratios**\n\nI’m working through some numbers mentally. So, I have c as .685715 and d as .314285. I think the ratio is around 2.181, but let me recalculate. Multiplying .314285 by that ratio gives me approximately .685, and multiplying it by 2 gives me about .62857. When I look at that .182 term, it seems to add around .057197, totaling up to about 2.181835.\n\nThen, I consider the odds — maybe they’re close to 3.375. If I take that further, multiplying gives me 11.390625, and I think the final multiplication would lead to around 57.665.",
